WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:01.878
<v Examiner>Case L.

2
00:00:01.878 --> 00:00:04.154
<v Examiner>Jack's noise-induced hearing loss.

3
00:00:04.154 --> 00:00:04.823
<v Examiner>Six marks.

4
00:00:05.273 --> 00:00:07.492
<v Examiner>Jack works in a very noisy open-plan office,

5
00:00:07.492 --> 00:00:10.519
<v Examiner>and for years he has listened to loud music to drown it out.

6
00:00:10.519 --> 00:00:12.788
<v Examiner>Now he has trouble hearing and understanding.

7
00:00:12.788 --> 00:00:15.562
<v Examiner>Which parts of the ear are damaged, and where are they?

8
00:00:15.562 --> 00:00:19.496
<v Examiner>How does loud noise over time damage them, and how does that lead to deafness?

9
00:00:19.496 --> 00:00:23.279
<v Examiner>What kind of deafness is this, and how does it compare with the other kind?

10
00:00:23.729 --> 00:00:27.187
This is one of the two case studies she names for Test 3,

11
00:00:27.187 --> 00:00:29.311
so treat it as close to guaranteed.

12
00:00:29.311 --> 00:00:30.949
It's four questions in one.

13
00:00:30.949 --> 00:00:33.862
Answer them in order, and that's your six marks.

14
00:00:34.312 --> 00:00:37.158
The hook: Corti, snap, silent, sensorineural.

15
00:00:37.158 --> 00:00:41.207
The hairs in the organ of Corti snap off, the nerve goes silent,

16
00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:43.357
and that's sensorineural deafness.

17
00:00:43.807 --> 00:00:46.411
First: which parts are damaged, and where?

18
00:00:46.411 --> 00:00:47.341
The hair cells.

19
00:00:47.341 --> 00:00:52.115
They're the mechanoreceptors for hearing, and they sit in the organ of Corti.

20
00:00:52.115 --> 00:00:54.904
Be specific, because the case asks you to be.

21
00:00:54.904 --> 00:00:59.244
The organ of Corti sits on the basilar membrane, in the cochlear duct,

22
00:00:59.244 --> 00:01:03.150
which is the scala media, inside the cochlea, in the inner ear.

23
00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:07.179
Hair cells, organ of Corti, basilar membrane, cochlea, inner ear.

24
00:01:07.179 --> 00:01:08.605
Give the whole address.

25
00:01:10.055 --> 00:01:12.737
Second: how does loud noise damage them?

26
00:01:12.737 --> 00:01:15.553
Loud sound is a wave with a big amplitude.

27
00:01:15.553 --> 00:01:21.319
The basilar membrane moves so hard that the hairs bend against the tectorial membrane,

28
00:01:21.319 --> 00:01:23.733
over and over, until they break off.

29
00:01:23.733 --> 00:01:25.811
Her deck gives you two numbers.

30
00:01:25.811 --> 00:01:30.504
Continuous exposure to eighty-five decibels or more does it over time.

31
00:01:30.504 --> 00:01:34.125
A hundred and thirty decibels or more does it at once.

32
00:01:34.125 --> 00:01:37.008
Jack is the over-time kind: years of noise.

33
00:01:37.458 --> 00:01:39.771
Third: how does that cause deafness?

34
00:01:39.771 --> 00:01:44.462
A hair cell's job is transduction: turning vibration into nerve impulses.

35
00:01:44.462 --> 00:01:46.454
A broken hair cell can't do it.

36
00:01:46.454 --> 00:01:51.401
So no impulses travel along the vestibulocochlear nerve, cranial nerve eight,

37
00:01:51.401 --> 00:01:54.228
to the auditory cortex in the temporal lobe.

38
00:01:54.228 --> 00:01:56.220
And hair cells don't grow back.

39
00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.983
So noise-induced hearing loss is permanent.

40
00:01:59.433 --> 00:02:02.687
Fourth: what kind of deafness, and the other kind?

41
00:02:02.687 --> 00:02:04.705
This is sensorineural deafness:

42
00:02:04.705 --> 00:02:09.520
damage anywhere from the hair cells of the cochlea to the auditory cortex.

43
00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:11.212
It's usually irreversible.

44
00:02:11.212 --> 00:02:15.247
Depending on where the damage is, a cochlear implant can help.

45
00:02:15.247 --> 00:02:17.720
The other kind is conduction deafness.

46
00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:23.382
Sound is blocked before it reaches the inner ear, somewhere in the outer or middle ear.

47
00:02:23.382 --> 00:02:24.423
Impacted earwax.

48
00:02:24.423 --> 00:02:25.790
A perforated eardrum.

49
00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:28.263
A middle ear infection, like glue ear.

50
00:02:28.263 --> 00:02:30.866
Conduction deafness is often reversible.

51
00:02:32.316 --> 00:02:34.076
Where the marks go missing.

52
00:02:34.076 --> 00:02:39.032
One: the semicircular canals have hair cells too, but those are for balance.

53
00:02:39.032 --> 00:02:42.227
Her own question offers them as the wrong answer.

54
00:02:42.227 --> 00:02:45.096
Jack's broken hair cells are in the cochlea.

55
00:02:45.096 --> 00:02:50.833
Two: the sound came in through a perfectly normal ear canal, so people write conduction.

56
00:02:50.833 --> 00:02:51.420
It isn't.

57
00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:53.115
Nothing blocked the sound.

58
00:02:53.115 --> 00:02:56.636
The damage is in the inner ear, so it's sensorineural.

59
00:02:56.636 --> 00:02:58.918
Three: don't stop at "the cochlea".

60
00:02:58.918 --> 00:03:01.722
Name the organ of Corti, and where it sits.

61
00:03:02.172 --> 00:03:02.761
<v Examiner>Once more.

62
00:03:02.761 --> 00:03:03.940
<v Examiner>Jack's hearing loss.

63
00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:06.887
<v Examiner>What's damaged, how, why he's deaf, and what kind.

64
00:03:09.337 --> 00:03:13.322
The hair cells of the organ of Corti, on the basilar membrane,

65
00:03:13.322 --> 00:03:15.379
in the cochlea of the inner ear.

66
00:03:15.379 --> 00:03:20.778
Loud, high-amplitude sound bends the hairs against the tectorial membrane until they

67
00:03:20.778 --> 00:03:25.406
break off: eighty-five decibels over time, a hundred and thirty at once.

68
00:03:25.406 --> 00:03:30.355
No impulses go down nerve eight to the brain, and hair cells don't grow back,

69
00:03:30.355 --> 00:03:31.512
so it's permanent.

70
00:03:31.512 --> 00:03:33.440
That's sensorineural deafness.

71
00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:37.875
The other kind, conduction, is a blockage in the outer or middle ear,

72
00:03:37.875 --> 00:03:41.153
like earwax or glue ear, and it's often reversible.
